-- title:
--   Vanishing q-torsion and line alignment at supersingular places
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $A_0$ denotes the valuation ring $A \cap k_0$, formally `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`, and $\kappa$ its residue field.
--
--   *Arithmetic data and reduction frame.* Fixed are a prime $q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`), a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a nonunit of $A$ (`hA`). A finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over the residue field of $A$ is assumed (`hW`) to consist exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, and $s$ is an element of $W$. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion of intermediate fields `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'`, i.e. the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-coefficient Laurent series of the full level-$M'$ modular function field is contained in the corresponding base change of the function field of level $q^2M'$ with group `levelH q M'`. Next, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`: it consists of a valuation subring `integers` of the source, a surjective ring homomorphism `residue` onto the target whose kernel is the maximal ideal of `integers`, and a map `placeMap` on places, subject to the compatibilities in that structure (membership of constants, compatibility of `residue` with the residue map of $A$, existence for each nonzero $f$ of a constant $c$ with $c \cdot f$ in `integers` and nonzero residue, preservation of the degree of places, and compatibility of `placeMap` with divisors of functions). The hypothesis `hR₀` requires in addition that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that image lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue is, as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   *The small field.* $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element lying in $A$ (`hπ`). The group of hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` states that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$, that $A_0$ is a henselian local ring, and that $\kappa$ is algebraically closed; `hκ` states that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   *Roots of unity.* $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q\ell_g$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell_g))$. Write $u$ for the unit of $k_0$ determined by $\xi$; thus $u^q$ has order $\ell_g$ and $u^{\ell_g}$ has order $q$.
--
--   *Level and function field.* $H_1$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ required (`hH₁`) to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) — by definition the kernel of `ZMod.unitsMap (dvd_sq_mul q M')` — with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $k_0$ in the Laurent series field over $k_0$, required (`hK`) to be `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`, the field generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise images of the $\mathbb Q$-rational function field of level $q^2M'$ and group $H_1$. The element $j \in K$ is required (`hj`) to be the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), and is nonzero. Algebra and scalar-tower instances relating $A_0$, $k_0$ and $K$ are present.
--
--   *Equivariance of the level conditions.* For every commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and every variable change $C$: `hℓ` says that `IsGamma1Point W ℓg D` (the level-$p$ datum $D$ satisfies the affine equation at $(x_P,y_P)$, annihilates the division polynomial `preΨ ℓg` at $x_P$, and has $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$) is preserved by passing to $C \bullet W$ and `D.variableChange C`; `hM` says that `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` is preserved on replacing $h$ by `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`; and `hL` says that a divisor $h$ of `inLineMulPoly W ℓg n x` transports, after `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d`, to a divisor of `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓg n (u_C^{-2}(x - C.r))`.
--
--   *Group laws and Drinfeld transport.* $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀` of relative group laws on the projective models of Weierstrass curves with unit discriminant over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`: each group law admits a points evaluation) and to have the origin as identity (`h𝒢O`: the identity section is cut out by a homomorphism from the origin chart ring killing the coordinates `xOverY` and `zOverY`). $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q`, that is, functorial transport of raw Drinfeld $q$-level pairs along $A_0$-algebra maps and variable changes preserving the level condition, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`, two clauses stating that the transported sections agree with the original ones after the graded maps realising variable changes and coefficient maps). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` supply, for every commutative $A_0$-algebra and every projective Weierstrass curve, graded ring homomorphisms between the graded rings `projModelGradingCR` realising a given variable change, respectively a given coefficient homomorphism, together with the requirement that the irrelevant ideal of the target be contained in the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source, and satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom`, respectively `IsCoefficientHom`.
--
--   *Moduli package and the Tate point.* Write $R$ for the rigid Weierstrass datum `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯`, obtained by restricting the product of the $\Gamma_0$-prime-power component for $M'$, the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ component and the Drinfeld $q$-level component along the link condition [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Link`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L26); a raw point of $R$ over a ring consists of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with level data `level` whose first factor is the family of prime-power kernel polynomials, whose second factor's first entry is a `LevelPData`, and whose second factor's second entry is a Drinfeld pair with sections $P$ and $Q$. Then $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for the associated level moduli datum: a ring $B_0$ with a universal point `P₀.univ`, such that every point over an $A_0$-algebra is the image of `P₀.univ` under a unique $A_0$-algebra map. Further, $x_0$ is a point of this datum over $K$ with $j(x_0)$ equal, as a Laurent series, to `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` (`hx₀`), and $\mathrm{clC}$ is an $A_0$-algebra map from $B_0$ to the chart subalgebra $\mathcal C :=$ `chartAlgFin A₀ ↥K j` $=$ `chartAlg A₀ ↥K {j}` of $K$ whose composite with the inclusion $\mathcal C \subseteq K$ is the classifying map `P₀.classify x₀` (`hclC`).
--
--   The hypothesis `hμ` pins $x_0$ to the Tate curve: there exist a variable change $C$ over the Laurent series field of $k_0$ and a raw point $r$ of $R$ over $K$ such that the class of $r$ is $x_0$ and, writing $(X,Y) =$ `tateToricPoint ↥k₀ q (u ^ q)`: (i) $C.u \cdot (2X + \tfrac16) = 2Y + X$ with constants taken as constant Hahn series, and $C.r = -\tfrac1{12}$, $C.s = -\tfrac12$, $C.t = \tfrac1{24}$; (ii) the base change of $r$'s curve along $K \hookrightarrow$ `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` equals $C \bullet$ `tateBase ↥k₀ q`; (iii) for every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every field $F'$, every ring homomorphism $f : k_0 \to F'$ and every primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta \in F'$, the image of the polynomial $r.\mathrm{level}_1(p)$ in the Laurent series field, followed by the coefficient map induced by $f$, equals `kernelVariableChangeDeg` of the variable change $C$ transported by $f$, in degree `gamma0PowDeg p (M'.factorization p)`, applied to $\prod_a (X - (\mathrm{toricPoint}\ F'\ q\ (\zeta^a))_1)$, the product over $1 \le a \le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p \nmid a$; (iv) the image of the `LevelPData` $r.\mathrm{level}_{2,1}$ in the Laurent series field equals the variable change by $C$ of the datum $(x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q) = (X,Y,X,Y)$; and (v) there exist $P_x,P_y,Q_x,Q_y \in K$ whose images in the Laurent series field are the four coordinates of the variable change by $C$ of `cuspData ↥k₀ q (u ^ ℓg) ![1,0] ![0,-1]`, and such that `IsSectionThrough r.level.2.2.P Px Py` and `IsSectionThrough r.level.2.2.Q Qx Qy` hold, i.e. each Drinfeld section is cut out by a homomorphism from the $Z$-chart ring with the indicated affine coordinates.
--
--   *Hover condition.* For a maximal ideal $y$ of $\mathcal C$, let $\mathrm{Hov}(y)$ denote the following condition: for every Laurent series $g$ over $\mathbb Q$ in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `R₀.integers`, such that $g$ has nonnegative order at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at which the base change of `jq` has nonnegative order, and such that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of the place $s$, and for every witness that the coefficientwise image of $g$ in the Laurent series field of $k_0$ lies in $K$ and that the resulting element of $K$ lies in $\mathcal C$: for every $c \in k_0$ lying in $A$ whose residue in the residue field of $A$ equals `s.evalAt` of the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the difference in $\mathcal C$ between that element and the image of $c$ under $A_0 \to \mathcal C$ lies in $y$.
--
--   *Conclusion.* Two assertions hold.
--
--   First: for every maximal ideal $y$ of $\mathcal C$ and every $A_0$-algebra map $\psi : \mathcal C \to \kappa$ whose zero set is exactly $y$, if $\mathrm{Hov}(y)$ holds, then for every raw point $x$ of $R$ over $\kappa$ whose class is the image of `P₀.univ` under $\psi \circ \mathrm{clC}$, every point $P$ of the affine curve $x.\mathrm{curve}$ with $q \cdot P = 0$ is the point at infinity; that is, the specialised curve has no nontrivial $q$-torsion over $\kappa$.
--
--   Second: for all maximal ideals $y, y'$ of $\mathcal C$ and all $A_0$-algebra maps $\psi, \psi' : \mathcal C \to \kappa$ whose zero sets are exactly $y$, respectively $y'$, if $\mathrm{Hov}(y)$ and $\mathrm{Hov}(y')$ hold, then for all raw points $x, x'$ of $R$ over $\kappa$ whose classes are the images of `P₀.univ` under $\psi \circ \mathrm{clC}$, respectively $\psi' \circ \mathrm{clC}$, there exists a variable change $C_0$ over $\kappa$ such that the curve of $R.\mathrm{act}\, C_0\, x$ equals the curve of $x'$, the first level component of $R.\mathrm{act}\, C_0\, x$ equals that of $x'$, and `InLine` holds for the curve of $x'$ at $\ell_g$ with first argument $x'.\mathrm{level}_{2,1}.x_P$ and second argument $(R.\mathrm{act}\, C_0\, x).\mathrm{level}_{2,1}.x_P$: explicitly, there is an integer $a$ with $1 \le a \le (\ell_g - 1)/2$ such that
--   $$(R.\mathrm{act}\, C_0\, x).\mathrm{level}_{2,1}.x_P \cdot (\Psi_a^2)(x'.\mathrm{level}_{2,1}.x_P) = \Phi_a(x'.\mathrm{level}_{2,1}.x_P),$$
--   the division polynomials being those of the curve of $x'$.
--
--   This is the supersingular-fibre reading step for the integral model of the modular curve of level $q^2M'$ with group $H_1 = \mathrm{levelH}(q,M') \cap \Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-type congruence conditions: over a maximal ideal of the finite chart that is directed at a supersingular place $s$ the specialised moduli point carries a curve with trivial $q$-torsion, and any two such specialisations agree, up to variable change, in the curve and the $\Gamma_0(M')$-kernel data, with their $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-points lying on one of the $(\ell_g-1)/2$ admissible lines. It is used in the construction of the supersingular fibre dictionary for the chart algebra at a directed maximal ideal.
